my name is sam bates i'm a koi pond  specialist at grassroots ponded garden  my family's business and today i'm going  to be showing you how we do a deep  cleaning on koi ponds  koi ponds can get pretty dirty and this  is for a number of different reasons one  of which at least here in michigan where  we are from we have a lot of deciduous  trees that drop leaves into the pond  they then break down that organic matter  can turn the water either brown or  sometimes it helps make it green this  one over here is what we call carpet  algae it's very very difficult to get  off the bottom and the sides of the  liner but it's actually beneficial to a  pond to have the carpet algae this  typically comes with age the carpet  algae can get as long as shag carpet and  also it houses bacteria and acts as  another biological filtration system  within your pond that you didn't even  have to pay for  when we are removing koi from ponds we  use what's called a sock net it's very  silky smooth so it's very delicate on  the fish and it has two open ends which  makes it easy to hold and the fish can't  escape and also you can release the fish  through the bottom or the back side of  the net so the fish really doesn't  undergo much stress during the  transportation process when we're  cleaning koi ponds we keep the koi  goldfish frogs and such in separate  collapsible containers they hold five to  six hundred gallons of water and leaves  plenty of room for the fish to hang out  ponds that are over taken with algae  whether it be microscopic algae which  causes green water or string algae  getting a deep clean to the very bottom  could be a good restart  this pond here represents what we  typically will find when it comes  springtime and time to open up a koi  pond the water is green that is the  suspended algae the ammonia breakdown  from any organic matter whether that be  sadly maybe a fish that passed away or  it can be tannins in the water which  leech from leaves pine cones sticks  anything like that and these things  should be cleaned out really once a year  we use this hose connected to typically  a 6 000 gallon per hour pump that we  submerge at the bottom of the pond to  remove the water  now where does the water go well it  really depends on where we're at  sometimes it goes to their yard  sometimes it goes out to the road it  really all just depends  some ponds only need to get pumped out  once those are the easiest and then  others it can be anywhere from two to a  dozen times when ponds are full of  gravel at the bottom you have to rinse  in between the gravel and you'll often  find a lot of mud and debris that's  hidden to your eye but we try to remove  it just because it can still affect with  water chemistry typically when cleaning  ponds we find all sorts of stuff but the  regular things that we find are  bullfrogs green frogs toads lots of  toads and we also find dragonfly larvae  turtles  in all of this mucky stuff that we find  at the bottom of ponds it ranges from  really a cocktail of tree leaves sorts  of needles and sticks and mostly fish  waste  and it smells  awful there's really no other way to put  it the  smell actually varies by pond  some  barely smell  and then some  make you just want to run away but you  have to go through it and you have to  get it out this pond was so large in  surface area with a flat bottom to where  we couldn't rinse everything to one end  we're using a squeegee to actually  remove all of the excess water and  debris that is stuck in the bottom of  the pond on their carpet algae  [Music]  the rock the liner can be covered in  string algae  if your lawnmower is aiming in the wrong  direction and it's kicking grass  clippings into the pond or sometimes  even sprays like herbicides or  pesticides can raise the ammonia levels  in the pond and that can cause just a  whole world of issues causing algae and  even killing some of the pets within the  system so you get as close as you can  with the right bit on the power washer  and you remove the algae then you rinse  it to the bottom and then we use a shop  vac to clean up all of the sludge and  nasty stuff that we left at the bottom  as well as vacuuming out the skimmers  and the filters  it's important to clean out the bottom  of the filtration system because this is  where all of the heavy sludge and fish  waste particularly like to set and stay  stagnant when refilling ponds after a  deep cleaning uh we typically just use  the client's garden hose and we will run  it until we have enough water for the  fish to be released we add dechlorinator  usually right at the beginning and we  will aerate the pond to ensure that the  dechlorinator has went thoroughly  through the existing water before we  release the fish we make sure that the  water is splashing into the pond opposed  to just filling from the bottom and  staying fairly stagnant creating those  bubbles helps push that chlorine out of  the system and gas off to make it  healthy for the fish  this bag of what looks to be dirty water  is actually beneficial bacteria that  helps break down fish waste and helps  with pond clarity throughout the season  this is something that we add weekly and  our clients often ask what is that bag  of brown water and what's in it all i  can tell them is it's bacteria because  that's my grandfather and my father's  secret recipe for years so i can't let  you in on that  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

